**Build: 0.11.411** (`93FB14589298C2E12BBAE2D`, was `B9EDF45FFDC01DA13BA8EE9`). Clean compile, no new warnings. **Still do not board** — 0.11.402..411 has never had a rig cycle.

I spent this session on the RULE-5 consult *before* writing the coordinator rather than after, and that was the right call: **GPT refuted the central design point.**

**What I proposed and what it costs.** My takeover precondition was "the sector at `owner_slot` no longer carries `{owner_node, owner_epoch}` as ACTIVE, therefore the owner incarnation is gone." GPT's refutation is sound and I accept it: `node_id` is an *identity*, not an *I/O capability*. The descriptor CAS and `owner_term` guard descriptor state; they do not stop an already-accepted SCSI command or a still-running replay worker whose buffer-image writes no auth recheck covers. Non-residency is admissible only as *retirement evidence*, and only when the transition producing it has a documented ordering guarantee — which none of them has in this tree today. Corruption and unread sectors are never evidence of departure.

It also caught a straight bug in the sess67 sketch: `!mxfs_disklock_slot_live(owner_slot)` indexes liveness **by slot, not by incarnation**. Once the slot is reclaimed it reads live again and takeover wedges permanently — and it is unconditionally true for our own slot, so occupying the old owner's slot would make takeover impossible forever.

**Landed:** `mxfs_disklock_slot_holds_incarnation()` — the disk-truth occupancy read the coordinator needs regardless (`find_node_slot` cannot answer it; its in-memory cache resolves a dead node forever). It fails closed on unread/bad-magic/alloc-failure. Its header carries the refutation explicitly so the next session cannot rebuild the wrong gate on top of it.

**Three further findings worth more than the coordinator itself:**
- The mount barrier replays *then* acquires. That ordering lets it replay concurrently with an existing descriptor owner and discover the conflict only afterwards. Acquisition must precede replay.
- `mxfs_disklock_purge_node`'s zeroing is the publication, and it is a plain zero rather than an exact-image CAS of the authorized `GRANTS_RELEASED` descriptor — so a stale owner could zero a successor's descriptor. That is a candidate **shipped** defect, not a design point; it needs auditing first.
- Every stage is at-least-once: an owner can complete a stage's external effect and die before `advance()`. Since we ruled replay idempotence may not be the contract, one coarse `IMAGES_REPLAYED` stage is insufficient — it needs durable per-item progress.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready — 14 open defects, 7 critical, and ten unboarded versions of change on the live death/recovery path.

